.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-exec-at-file-hangs-on-stdin-in-background.md:2:name: codex-exec-at-file-hangs-on-stdin-in-background
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-exec-at-file-hangs-on-stdin-in-background.md:3:description: "`codex exec \"@promptfile\"` launched via Bash run_in_background can hang reading stdin and exit 0 with zero output files; inline the prompt via \"$(cat file)\" and redirect stdin from /dev/null."
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-exec-at-file-hangs-on-stdin-in-background.md:10:tags: [process, codex, evaluation]
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-exec-at-file-hangs-on-stdin-in-background.md:11:domain: process
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-exec-at-file-hangs-on-stdin-in-background.md:16:# `codex exec "@promptfile"` can silently hang on stdin in a background Bash call
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-exec-at-file-hangs-on-stdin-in-background.md:20:The T04 iter2 Codex evaluation leg was launched as `timeout 600 codex exec --sandbox workspace-write --cd <worktree> "@<promptfile>" > log 2>&1` via `Bash(run_in_background:true)`. Codex exited 0 but wrote ZERO output files. The stdout log contained only `Reading additional input from stdin...` — codex was waiting on stdin (which, in a background Bash task, is not a TTY and never delivers the prompt), then terminated without producing the contracted per-perspective files. An identical-shaped iter1 invocation had worked, so the failure was intermittent/environment-dependent, which is worse — it passes sometimes and silently no-ops other times.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-exec-at-file-hangs-on-stdin-in-background.md:24:Assumed `"@promptfile"` is a reliable way to feed the prompt to `codex exec` in every context. In a non-interactive background invocation, codex's `@file` / prompt-arg handling fell through to reading additional input from stdin; with no stdin attached, it got EOF and produced nothing. The `> log 2>&1` redirect hid that it was waiting (no visible prompt), and `exit 0` made it look successful to the files-as-truth check only because we DID verify file existence (0 files) rather than trusting the exit code — which is the one thing that caught it.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-exec-at-file-hangs-on-stdin-in-background.md:29:`timeout 600 codex exec --sandbox workspace-write --cd <worktree> "$(cat <promptfile>)" < /dev/null > log 2>&1`
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-exec-at-file-hangs-on-stdin-in-background.md:30:The `"$(cat file)"` guarantees codex receives the prompt as its argument (no `@`-resolution / stdin fallback), and `< /dev/null` guarantees it cannot block waiting for stdin. Always run the files-as-truth check (file existence + content grep) after; on 0 files, re-run with this robust form rather than proceeding. Consider folding this into the [[codex]] skill's invocation-patterns section as the preferred non-interactive form.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/codex-exec-at-file-hangs-on-stdin-in-background.md:34:Symptoms: `codex exec` exits 0 but the contracted output directory is empty, and the stdout log's last line is `Reading additional input from stdin...`. Any time a `codex exec` produces no files despite exit 0, suspect the prompt never reached the model. The codex skill's "files-as-truth completion signal" rule is what surfaces this — never trust exit code alone.
