.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/session-dir-placed-outside-worktree.md:10:tags: [session-lifecycle, worktree, bootstrap, orchestration]
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/session-dir-placed-outside-worktree.md:12:domain: process
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/session-dir-placed-outside-worktree.md:31:The orchestration skill's Configuration Step 1 procedure (rows 5 and 6) specifies the session-file path as `.gobbi/projects/{project-name}/sessions/{date}-{session-id}/...` — a **relative** path with no explicit tree qualifier. The manager resolved the relative path against the current working directory (the main tree, where the session started), not against `session.json.git.worktreePath`.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/session-dir-placed-outside-worktree.md:33:The qualified absolute-root rule lives in `git/SKILL.md` § Memory Access Matrix (`**Critical rule — write paths**` at line 33) and bundle-B design doc `qualified-git-write-path-rule.md`. The manager either failed to load that rule before bootstrap, or loaded it but did not apply it to Configuration rows 5 and 6 because the orchestration rows themselves do not cite the qualifier inline.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/session-dir-placed-outside-worktree.md:50:The orchestration skill text (rows 5 and 6) must cite the qualified absolute-root rule inline with a direct pointer to `git/SKILL.md` § Memory Access Matrix and to `qualified-git-write-path-rule.md`.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/session-dir-placed-outside-worktree.md:57:- The orchestration skill's row 5 says "write `.gobbi/projects/{project-name}/sessions/…`" without a tree qualifier.
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/session-dir-placed-outside-worktree.md:64:- `codex-subprocess-writes-to-main-tree.md` — the session that surfaced this mistake also saw evaluator subagents (both Claude and Codex legs) write EVAL artifacts to the main tree; same root cause (CWD defaults to main tree, path macros aren't resolved by subagents).
.gobbi/projects/gobbi/mistakes/manager-context-overflow-with-large-bundle.md:10:tags: [process, orchestration, planning]
