+# Aesthetics Perspective
+
+## Artifact Summary
+What: execution-phase evaluation of the readability and style of the amended `orchestration/SKILL.md`. Why: this file is a high-traffic manager-facing contract, so the amendment must be scannable and use names that match the redesign. How: close-read the changed sections and grep for canonical terms, links, and stale wording.
+
+Memory reads: evaluation prompt; amended target file; plan T3 block; idea artifact sections defining "per-task slice", the correction pattern, and `workflow.chat.tasks[]`; relevant mistakes on naming, section order, and docs-sync; `evaluation/SKILL.md`; `execution/evaluation.md`. Prior iter: none.
+
+## Locked Frame (Stage 1)
+Scenario A1: Names and terms match the locked redesign.
+Checklist: `per-task slice` appears where the Chat branch is summarized; `workflow.chat.tasks[]` is used consistently; links are Markdown links, not bare prose only.
+
+Scenario A2: The correction pattern is readable.
+Checklist: original sentence is preserved with strikethrough; correction prose is adjacent; the retained first sentence is distinguished from the superseded second sentence.
+
+Scenario A3 (adversarial): A polished-looking table hides stale local prose.
+Checklist: table captions and lead-in counts agree with the rows they introduce; old gate counts are not left behind.
+
+## Stage 2 Findings
+A1 checks: Yes. `rg` found `per-task slice` in lines 66, 70, 87, 89, and 243, and `workflow.chat.tasks[]` in lines 362, 363, and 469.
+
+A2 checks: Yes. Line 247 preserves `Mode controls user gates; it does not relax the workflow.` wrapped in `~~...~~` and appends the dated correction.
+
+A3 checks: No, but the defect is recorded under the stronger Consistency/Usage lenses as COD-CONS-001 and COD-USAGE-001. Aesthetics does not add a separate finding because the failure mode is semantic count drift, not naming or formatting alone.
+
+No Aesthetics-only findings.
+
+## Verdict
+VERDICT: PASS
